#
# \brief  Sponge DE base-sel4 interactive GUI scenario (Phase 10 W2)
# \author Sponge OS contributors
# \date   2026
#
# Closes the deferred follow-up #1 in docs/09-roadmap.md §11 (the REAL
# PC hardware driver set wired into a base-sel4 QEMU run script) AND
# delivers the Phase 10 W2 proof of roadmap §10 criteria 3 (click-to-
# launch) + 4 (panel interactions) over the real QMP input path.
#
# What this scenario wires (the vendored Genode
# recipes/raw/drivers_interactive-pc driver set, built from source and
# inlined because Sponge OS does not use the depot):
#
#   init
#   + timer
#   + report_rom        (relays sponge-de's "input" report to the probe;
#                        wires request/result/launcher_request/
#                        launcher_result/installed/runtime between
#                        sponge_pkgd, sponge-de, and the probe)
#   + nitpicker         (Gui/Capture/Event; the compositor + event sink)
#   + drivers           (sub-init; managing_system; routes Capture/Event
#                        to nitpicker and Timer to timer). Internally it
#                        runs the canonical interactive-PC set:
#                          acpi -> report_rom -> pci_decode -> platform
#                          vesa_fb  (Capture <- nitpicker, the display)
#                          ps2      (relative pointer + keyboard)
#                          usb      = pc_usb_host (the xHCI HCD, -device
#                                    nec-usb-xhci in QEMU)
#                          usb_hid  (binds the -device usb-tablet)
#                          event_filter (provides Event; merges ps2+usb,
#                                    chargen/accel; re-submits to
#                                    nitpicker via the sub-init route)
#   + sponge-de         (Qt6 DE; panel + launcher popup + themed demo
#                       window). Renders on base-sel4 since the caps fix
#                       (header note below). Launcher backend wired to
#                       sponge_pkgd (Phase 10 W2): reads the installed
#                       broadcast, writes launcher_request on click,
#                       reads launcher_result.
#   + sponge_pkgd       (package backend; owns pkg_runtime config;
#                       answers launcher_request via _do_launch — same
#                       backend as vct launch)
#   + pkg_runtime       (nested init hosting launched packages; caps 2000
#                       / ram 256M for Qt6/Mesa softpipe on seL4)
#   + sponge_de_probe   (inject=no, render_iters=1800,
#                       phases=input,panel,launch; proves the themed
#                       window via Capture pixel check, the input round-
#                       trip via real QMP clicks, the panel S-toggle
#                       popup open/close, and click-to-launch of
#                       pkg_gui_demo — all over the real PS/2 mouse
#                       driver path (PS/2 → ps2 → event_filter →
#                       nitpicker → sponge-de) → "PASS").
#
# AUTOMATED VERIFICATION (what gates "Run script execution successful"):
#
#   Two boot-log assertions prove the driver stack that roadmap §11 item
#   1 actually asks for, and a series of QMP-driven gates prove the
#   Phase 10 W2 criteria 3 + 4 over the real input chain:
#     (1) [drivers -> fb] "using 1024x768"  — vesa_fb set the mode and
#         mapped the physical VESA framebuffer (it is consuming
#         nitpicker's Capture, i.e. driving the real display).
#     (2) [drivers -> usb_hid] "Connected device: ... QEMU QEMU USB
#         Tablet ... POINTER" — usb_hid bound the absolute pointer. This
#         is the last driver up, so it also implies acpi -> pci_decode
#         -> platform -> pc_usb_host (xHCI) all came up before it.
#     (3) PHASE INPUT (criterion 1) — QMP PS/2-mouse click at the
#         demo window center; sponge-de's input report confirms the
#         press. Proves the PS/2 → ps2 → event_filter → nitpicker →
#         sponge-de chain.
#     (4) PHASE PANEL (criterion 4) — QMP PS/2-mouse click on the
#         panel S toggle opens the launcher popup (Capture non-bg
#         fraction in POPUP_RECT rises). Then a QMP click on the
#         demo window body closes the popup via focus-out auto-close.
#         FATAL — both observations must succeed.
#     (5) PHASE LAUNCH (criterion 3) — first, install pkg_gui_demo via
#         the request channel (mirrors run/sponge-launch.run's
#         launch_probe pattern, populates the launcher menu); then QMP
#         click on S to open the popup; then QMP click on the first
#         launcher entry → sponge-de's LauncherController →
#         launcher_request report → pkgd _do_launch → pkg_runtime
#         config regen → pkg_gui_demo boot → green #00ff00 first
#         paint. FATAL.
#     (6) INDEPENDENT CORROBORATION — pkg_gui_demo's "window shown"
#         boot marker (the misleading_success_output defense).
#     (7) FINAL GATE — sponge-de-probe: PASS.
#
# Qt6/Mesa (EGL) on base-sel4 — RESOLVED (was: known limitation,
# docs/09-roadmap.md §11.1): sponge-de's Qt6/EGL init used to block on
# seL4. Root cause: capability exhaustion. base-sel4's cap accounting is
# roughly 3x base-linux's per operation (each Genode capability maps to
# several raw seL4 caps), so the former `caps: 300` ran out in the
# middle of Mesa's screen creation. The child requested a tiny upgrade
# (`upgrading quota donation for PD session (0 bytes, 4 caps)`) and then
# blocked *silently* — no error, no log, an infinite hang inside
# EGL/softpipe init. `caps: 1000` gives comfortable headroom (measured:
# 300 hangs at EGL init; 1000 renders the full desktop; RAM was never
# the bottleneck — the upgrade request was 0 bytes, so `ram: 128M`
# matches the base-linux scenarios). With that, sponge-de renders and
# the probe's Capture pixel check + QMP clicks pass.
#
# Automated, headless, no human in the loop: -nographic (serial only).
# To drive the desktop with a real mouse (control escape hatch,
# AGENTS.md §1.1), see the "Interactive viewing" note at the bottom of
# this file.
#
# This scenario is base-sel4 only: vesa_fb/ps2/usb_hid need real PC
# hardware access that base-linux does not provide (base-linux uses
# fb_sdl — see run/sponge-de.run for that variant). base-hw/base-nova
# are plausible but untested.
#

assert {[have_spec sel4]} \
	"sponge-de-sel4-interactive requires KERNEL=sel4 (BOARD=pc) in build.conf. \
	 This scenario drives real PC hardware (vesa_fb/ps2/usb_hid) which \
	 base-linux cannot provide; use run/sponge-de.run for the fb_sdl variant."

build { core lib/ld init timer server/report_rom server/nitpicker \
         driver/acpi app/pci_decode driver/platform \
         driver/framebuffer/vesa driver/ps2 \
         driver/usb_host/pc driver/usb_hid \
         server/event_filter \
         sponge_pkgd sponge-de test/pkg_gui_demo test/sponge_de_probe \
         qt6/base lib/libc lib/libm lib/stdcxx lib/mesa \
         lib/mesa_gpu-softpipe lib/egl lib/softpipe lib/libdrm lib/expat \
         lib/qt6_component lib/vfs lib/vfs_pipe lib/vfs_gpu lib/rom_filter \
         lib/zlib lib/libpng lib/blit }

create_boot_directory

install_config {
config
+ parent-provides
  + service ROM
  + service PD
  + service RM
  + service CPU
  + service LOG
  + service IRQ
  + service IO_MEM
  + service IO_PORT

+ default | caps: 200 | ram: 4M
+ default-route
  + any-service
    + parent
    + any-child

+ start timer
  + provides | + service Timer

+ start report_rom | caps: 300 | ram: 8M
  + provides
    + service Report
    + service ROM
  + config | verbose: no
    #
    # Input report relay (W1 criterion 1): sponge-de -> input → probe.
    #
    + policy | label: sponge_de_probe -> sponge_de_input | report: sponge-de -> input
    #
    # pkgd's "request" ROM — this scenario has no vct/launch_probe to drive
    # the request channel, but pkgd opens the ROM at startup. report_rom
    # requires a policy to serve the session (otherwise the ROM is denied
    # and pkgd stops). Map to a non-existent report label → empty ROM →
    # pkgd stays idle (same behavior as sponge-launcher.run before
    # launch_probe writes its first request).
    #
    + policy | label: sponge_pkgd -> request             | report: sponge_de_probe -> request
    + policy | label: sponge_de_probe -> result          | report: sponge_pkgd -> result
    #
    # Launcher channel (W2 criteria 3,4): pkgd <-> sponge-de over the
    # dedicated launcher_request / launcher_result labels (single-writer
    # per label — the long-lived launcher cannot share vct's request
    # label). Mirrors run/sponge-launch.run lines 91-99.
    #
    + policy | label: sponge_pkgd -> launcher_request  | report: sponge_de_probe -> launcher_request
    + policy | label: sponge-de -> launcher_result     | report: sponge_pkgd -> launcher_result
    #
    # pkgd's installed-set broadcast → sponge-de (populates the launcher
    # menu).
    #
    + policy | label: sponge-de -> installed           | report: sponge_pkgd -> installed
    #
    # pkg_runtime's config ROM ← pkgd's runtime report (pkgd owns the
    # nested init's config; every install/launch regenerates it).
    #
    + policy | label: pkg_runtime -> config            | report: sponge_pkgd -> runtime

+ start nitpicker | ram: 16M
  + provides
    + service Gui
    + service Capture
    + service Event
  + route
    + service Report | + child report_rom
    + service Timer  | + child timer
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child
  + config
    + capture
    + event
    + domain panel | layer: 1 | content: client | label: no
                   | hover: always | focus: click | ypos: 0 | height: 28
    + domain launcher | layer: 4 | content: client | label: no
                      | hover: always | focus: click
                      | xpos: 0 | ypos: 28 | width: 341 | height: 480
    + domain demo | layer: 2 | content: client | label: no
                  | hover: always | focus: click
                  | xpos: 192 | ypos: 172 | width: 640 | height: 480
    + domain default | layer: 3 | content: client | label: no
                     | hover: always | focus: click | ypos: 28 | height: -28
    + policy | label_prefix: sponge-de -> Sponge Panel   | domain: panel
    + policy | label_prefix: sponge-de -> Sponge Launcher | domain: launcher
    + policy | label_prefix: sponge-de -> Sponge DE Demo | domain: demo
    + default-policy | domain: default
    + background | color: #1e1e2e

+ start drivers | caps: 1500 | ram: 96M | managing_system: yes
  + binary init
  + route
    + service ROM | label: config | + parent | label: drivers.config
    + service Timer               | + child timer
    + service Capture             | + child nitpicker
    + service Event               | + child nitpicker
    + any-service                 | + parent
  + config
    + parent-provides
      + service IRQ
      + service IO_MEM
      + service IO_PORT
      + service ROM
      + service PD
      + service RM
      + service CPU
      + service LOG
      + service Timer
      + service Capture
      + service Event

    + default | caps: 60

    + start acpi | caps: 250 | ram: 4M
      + route
        + service IO_MEM | + parent
        + service LOG    | + parent
        + service PD     | + parent
        + service RM     | + parent
        + service CPU    | + parent
        + service ROM    | + parent
        + service Report | + child report_rom

    + start report_rom | caps: 100 | ram: 2M
      + provides
      | + service ROM
      | + service Report
      + config
      | + policy | label: pci_decode -> system | report: acpi -> acpi
      | + policy | label: platform -> devices  | report: pci_decode -> devices
      | + policy | label: usb_hid -> report    | report: usb -> devices
      + route
        + service LOG | + parent
        + service PD  | + parent
        + service CPU | + parent
        + service ROM | + parent

    + start pci_decode | caps: 100 | ram: 2M
      + route
        + service Report              | + child report_rom
        + service ROM | label: system | + child report_rom
        + service IO_MEM              | + parent
        + service LOG                 | + parent
        + service PD                  | + parent
        + service RM                  | + parent
        + service CPU                 | + parent
        + service ROM                 | + parent

    + start platform | caps: 200 | ram: 3M | managing_system: yes
      + provides
      | + service Dma
      | + service Platform
      + route
      | + service ROM | label: devices | + child report_rom
      | + service Report               | + child report_rom
      | + service IRQ                  | + parent
      | + service IO_MEM               | + parent
      | + service IO_PORT              | + parent
      | + service ROM                  | + parent
      | + service PD                   | + parent
      | + service CPU                  | + parent
      | + service LOG                  | + parent
      | + service Timer                | + parent
      + config
        + report | devices: yes
        + policy | label_prefix: ps2             | + device ps2
        + policy | label_prefix: fb  | info: yes | + pci | class: VGA
        + policy | label_prefix: usb | info: yes | + pci | class: USB

    + start fb | caps: 150 | ram: 20M
      + binary vesa_fb
      + route
        + service ROM | label: config | + parent | label: fb.config
        + service Report              | + child report_rom
        + service Dma                 | + child platform
        + service Platform            | + child platform
        + service IO_PORT             | + parent
        + service IO_MEM              | + parent
        + service ROM                 | + parent
        + service PD                  | + parent
        + service CPU                 | + parent
        + service LOG                 | + parent
        + service Timer               | + parent
        + service Capture             | + parent

    + start ps2 | caps: 100 | ram: 1280K
      + config
      + route
        + service Dma      | + child platform
        + service Platform | + child platform
        + service Event    | + child event_filter | label: ps2
        + service ROM      | + parent
        + service PD       | + parent
        + service CPU      | + parent
        + service LOG      | + parent
        + service Timer    | + parent

    + start usb | caps: 200 | ram: 16M
      + binary pc_usb_host
      + provides | + service Usb
      + config
      | + report | devices: yes
      | + policy | label_prefix: usb_hid | + device | class: 0x3
      + route
        + service Dma                     | + child platform
        + service Platform                | + child platform
        + service Report | label: devices | + child report_rom
        + service ROM                     | + parent
        + service PD                      | + parent
        + service RM                      | + parent
        + service CPU                     | + parent
        + service LOG                     | + parent
        + service Timer                   | + parent

    + start usb_hid | caps: 180 | ram: 10M
      + route
        + service ROM | label: report | + child report_rom
        + service ROM                 | + parent
        + service PD                  | + parent
        + service RM                  | + parent
        + service CPU                 | + parent
        + service LOG                 | + parent
        + service Timer               | + parent
        + service Event               | + child event_filter | label: usb
        + service Usb                 | + child usb

    + start event_filter | caps: 90 | ram: 1280K
      + provides | + service Event
      + route
        + service ROM | label: config | + parent | label: event_filter.config
        + service Event               | + parent
        + service ROM                 | + parent
        + service PD                  | + parent
        + service CPU                 | + parent
        + service LOG                 | + parent
        + service Timer               | + parent

# sponge_pkgd — package backend. Owns pkg_runtime's config; answers
# launches from BOTH the request channel (vct) and the launcher_request
# channel (sponge-de's click-to-launch). The <launcher_request/> config
# node enables the launcher channel (sponge_pkgd/main.cc:1610-1627);
# _do_launch (main.cc:1104) is shared by both channels, proving
# AGENTS.md §3.3 rule 5 (two interfaces, one backend) over the real
# input path. No <vfs> node → no persistence session (in-memory store,
# same as sponge-launch.run).
#
+ start sponge_pkgd | caps: 200 | ram: 16M
  + config
    + launcher_request
  + route
    + service ROM | label_last: request           | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: launcher_request   | + child report_rom
    + service Report                       | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

#
# pkg_runtime — nested init hosting launched packages. Its config is
# regenerated by pkgd (relayed via report_rom "runtime" → "config").
# caps: 2000 / ram: 256M: Qt6 GUI child on seL4 needs the §11.1 cap
# headroom (base-sel4's 3x cap accounting); pkgd's generator already
# emits the 1000-cap GUI floor per-package. Gui routes to nitpicker
# directly (no wm in this scenario — the plan's topology decision);
# launched GUI packages resolve via any-service → parent → nitpicker,
# landing in the default domain.
#
+ start pkg_runtime | caps: 2000 | ram: 256M
  + binary init
  + route
    + service ROM | label: config                 | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label_last: mesa_gpu.lib.so   | + parent | label: mesa_gpu-softpipe.lib.so
    + service Gui                                 | + child nitpicker
    + service Report                              | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

# caps: 1000 (not the base-linux 300): base-sel4's capability accounting
# is roughly 3x base-linux's; 300 caps run out inside Mesa's EGL screen
# creation and the component hangs *silently* (see the header note).
#
# sponge_de_probe inject=no: the inline <config> is delivered to the
# child by init's sandbox Inline_config_rom_service. Since Genode 26.05,
# the sandbox delivers child configs in HID format by default (the
# dynamic linker's `_generate_xml' config switch defaults to false — see
# genode/repos/base/src/lib/ldso/main.cc). The probe reads its config
# via the modern Genode::Node API (since this workstream), which
# transparently accepts both HID and XML; the inject="no" attribute
# round-trips correctly in either format. (The earlier probe code used
# `_config.xml().attribute_value(...)' which returns <empty/> on HID
# input, so inject silently defaulted to true — the W0 unexpected-green
# root cause; see docs/evidence/task-0-phase10-interactive.log.)
+ start sponge-de | caps: 1000 | ram: 128M
  + config
    + launcher | source: pkgd
    + vfs
      + dir dev
        + log
        + inline rtc | : 2018-01-01 00:01
      + dir pipe | + pipe
      + tar qt6_dejavusans.tar
      + tar qt6_libqgenode.tar
    + libc | stdout: /dev/log | stderr: /dev/log | pipe: /pipe | rtc: /dev/rtc
  + route
    + service Gui | label_last: Sponge Panel   | + child nitpicker
    + service Gui | label_last: Sponge Launcher | + child nitpicker
    + service Gui                              | + child nitpicker
    + service Report | label: shape  | + child report_rom
    + service Report | label: input  | + child report_rom
    + service Report | label: launcher          | + child report_rom
    + service Report | label: launcher_request  | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: installed            | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: launcher_result      | + child report_rom
    + service ROM | label: default.theme        | + parent
    + service ROM | label: mesa_gpu.lib.so | + parent | label: mesa_gpu-softpipe.lib.so
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child

+ start sponge_de_probe | caps: 100 | ram: 16M
  + config | inject: no | render_iters: 1800 | phases: input,panel,launch
  + route
    + service Capture | + child nitpicker
    + service Timer   | + child timer
    + service ROM     | label: sponge_de_input | + child report_rom
    + service ROM     | label: result        | + child report_rom
    + service Report  | + child report_rom
    + any-service
      + parent
      + any-child
- }

#
# Stage every non-build-artifact boot module into bin/ BEFORE
# build_boot_image. On base-sel4 the boot image is a single packed
# image.elf (not a run_dir/genode/ directory of files like base-linux),
# so modules added after build_boot_image never reach the guest. The
# Genode run tool's build_boot_image resolves each listed module by name
# from bin/, so we copy the extras there and append them to the module
# list (same pattern as libports/run/qt6_common.inc's chargen staging).
#
exec mkdir -p bin

#
# qt6_dejavusans.tar is not a build output (the raw recipe is
# depot-only), so for a source build we generate it here from the
# DejaVuSans.ttf shipped in the qt6_base port sources (mirrors
# repos/libports/recipes/raw/qt6_dejavusans/).
#
set dejavu_dst "bin/qt6_dejavusans.tar"
if {![file exists $dejavu_dst]} {
	set dejavu_src [glob -nocomplain "${genode_dir}/contrib/qt6_base-*/src/lib/qt6_base/src/3rdparty/wasm/DejaVuSans.ttf"]
	if {[llength $dejavu_src] == 0} {
		error "DejaVuSans.ttf not found in the qt6_base port sources"
	}
	set staging "[run_dir]/dejavusans-staging"
	file mkdir "$staging/qt/lib/fonts"
	file copy -force [lindex $dejavu_src 0] "$staging/qt/lib/fonts/DejaVuSans.ttf"
	exec tar -cf $dejavu_dst -C $staging qt
	file delete -force $staging
}

#
# Driver sub-init config modules (the vendored
# recipes/raw/drivers_interactive-pc/ set; drivers.config is inlined as
# the <config> of the drivers start node above, fb.config is served to
# the fb child by label). Upstream stages these via the depot pkg; Sponge
# OS builds from source, so we copy them in directly.
#
set recipe_dir "${genode_dir}/repos/os/recipes/raw/drivers_interactive-pc"
foreach f { drivers.config fb.config } {
	if {![file exists "$recipe_dir/$f"]} {
		error "$recipe_dir/$f missing (vendored drivers_interactive-pc recipe drifted?)"
	}
	exec cp -f "$recipe_dir/$f" "bin/$f"
}

#
# event_filter.config — WRITTEN (not copied) with two deviations from
# the recipe:
#   1. The `accelerate' wrapper around the ps2 input is removed.
#      W3 evidence (docs/evidence/task-3-phase10-interactive.md) shows
#      rel-1 → ~10px/event with the standard `accelerate' wrapper, but
#      for the PS/2 click path we need a 1:1 (or at least tame) rel-
#      count-to-pixel mapping — the accelerator's 1000% sensitivity would
#      amplify the per-step error from a back-to-back burst of fine rel
#      events.
#   2. A <transform> scale on the usb input. QEMU 11's usb-tablet
#      reports absolute positions in device units (0..32767 per axis)
#      and nitpicker treats Absolute_motion values as raw PIXELS, so
#      unscaled tablet events clamp to the screen corner (observed:
#      (528,396) -> (1023,767)). The scale maps device units to the
#      1024x768 framebuffer (1024/32767, 768/32767); verified by the
#      W4 evidence event_filter log showing ABS_MOTION +528+396 for abs
#      (16896,16895). The PS/2 path is unchanged otherwise.
# The charger keyboard path (en_us/special includes, modifier and
# repeat nodes) is identical to the recipe. All staging happens BEFORE
# build_boot_image: on base-sel4 the boot image is a single packed
# image.elf, so modules added later never reach the guest
# (docs/11-environment.md §10.4).
#
set ef_fh [open "bin/event_filter.config" w]
puts $ef_fh {config
+ output
  + chargen
    + merge
    | + transform
    | | + scale | x: 0.0312517 | y: 0.0234382
    | | + input usb
    | + button-scroll
    | |   + input ps2
    | |   + vertical   | button: BTN_MIDDLE | speed_percent: -10
    | |   + horizontal | button: BTN_MIDDLE | speed_percent: -10
  + mod1
  | + key KEY_LEFTSHIFT
  | + key KEY_RIGHTSHIFT
  + mod2
  | + key KEY_LEFTCTRL
  | + key KEY_RIGHTCTRL
  + mod3
  | + key KEY_RIGHTALT | . AltGr
  + repeat | delay_ms: 230 | rate_ms: 90
  + include | rom: en_us.chargen
  + include | rom: special.chargen
+ policy | label: ps2 | input: ps2
+ policy | label: usb | input: usb
-}
close $ef_fh

#
# en_us.chargen + special.chargen — event_filter's keyboard character
# map (the recipe's `chargen <include rom: en_us.chargen>` requires
# them as boot modules). Sourced from the event_filter server sources.
#
set chargen_dir "${genode_dir}/repos/os/src/server/event_filter"
foreach f { en_us.chargen special.chargen } {
	if {![file exists "$chargen_dir/$f"]} {
		error "$chargen_dir/$f missing (event_filter sources drifted?)"
	}
	exec cp -f "$chargen_dir/$f" "bin/$f"
}

#
# Default theme ROM module (sponge-de opens it as "default.theme").
#
exec cp -f "${genode_dir}/repos/sponge/src/sponge-de/themes/default.theme" \
           "bin/default.theme"

#
# Stage package metadata + the derived index so sponge_pkgd can resolve
# pkg_gui_demo (the click-to-launch target). Same scheme as
# run/sponge-launch.run lines 198-213.
#
set staged_pkgs { pkg_gui_demo }
foreach pkg $staged_pkgs {
	set src "${genode_dir}/../pkg/$pkg/metadata.xml"
	if {![file exists $src]} {
		error "package metadata not found: $src"
	}
	exec cp -L $src "bin/pkg_$pkg.xml"
}

set index_fh [open "bin/pkg_index.xml" w]
puts $index_fh "<packages>"
foreach pkg $staged_pkgs {
	puts $index_fh "  <pkg name=\"$pkg\"/>"
}
puts $index_fh "</packages>"
close $index_fh

#
# Full boot module list. The Qt6 shared libs + qt6_libqgenode.tar are
# cmake-built and already symlinked into bin/ by the qt6/base target, so
# they are not in [build_artifacts] and must be listed explicitly
# (matching run/sponge-de-test.run's manual staging, but done before
# build_boot_image so the modules land inside image.elf on base-sel4).
#
set boot_modules [build_artifacts]
foreach m { libQt6Core.lib.so libQt6Gui.lib.so libQt6Widgets.lib.so \
            libqgenode.lib.so qt6_libqgenode.tar qt6_dejavusans.tar \
            drivers.config fb.config event_filter.config \
            en_us.chargen special.chargen \
            default.theme \
            pkg_pkg_gui_demo.xml pkg_index.xml } {
	lappend boot_modules $m
}

build_boot_image $boot_modules

#
# QEMU: base-sel4 needs >= 1 GiB guest RAM (seL4 SKIM window + kernel
# structures; see docs/11-environment.md §7.2). Bumped to 2G for the
# pkg_runtime nested init (256M) hosting a second Qt6/Mesa softpipe
# renderer (pkg_gui_demo) — same budget as run/sponge-alpha.run and
# run/sponge-launch.run. -nographic keeps it headless (serial only).
# The nec-usb-xhci + usb-tablet give an absolute pointer that usb_hid
# binds, matching the roadmap requirement.
#
# QMP control socket (`-qmp tcp:...,server=on,wait=off`): lets the run
# script drive host-side input events (`input-send-event`, `send-key`)
# through the real PS/2 mouse / usb-tablet device chain. QMP is
# appended to qemu_args ONLY before gate 3 (the host-driven click
# gate); the pre-click gates 1-2 spawn a plain QEMU and don't need it.
# The socket is non-blocking on the QEMU side (server=on,wait=off) so
# QEMU does not block waiting for us; qmp.inc::qmp_connect retries the
# connect on the Tcl side for the brief window between QEMU spawn and
# the listener being ready. See run/qmp.inc for the protocol helper.
#
# Source the shared QMP helper up front (Tcl 8.x builtin socket only;
# no external dependencies).
#
source [file join [file dirname [file normalize [info script]]] qmp.inc]

append qemu_args " -nographic -m 2G "
append qemu_args " -device nec-usb-xhci,id=xhci -device usb-tablet "

#
# Automated verification (roadmap §11, item 1: the interactive-PC driver
# set wired into a base-sel4 QEMU run; Phase 10 W2: criteria 3 + 4
# over the real QMP input path).
#
# This scenario proves the part that roadmap item actually asks for —
# the REAL driver set (vesa_fb, ps2, usb_hid, event_filter, platform,
# acpi, pci_decode, pc_usb_host) booting and binding hardware on
# base-sel4, including the usb-tablet absolute pointer — AND that real
# QMP-driven PS/2-mouse clicks traverse the full hardware input chain
# (QMP input-send-event → emulated PS/2 controller → ps2 driver →
# event_filter → nitpicker → sponge-de) end-to-end. Explicit gates:
#
#   (1) DISPLAY: vesa_fb sets the mode and maps the physical VESA
#       framebuffer (vesa_fb is consuming nitpicker's Capture session,
#       i.e. driving the real display with nitpicker's composited
#       output).
#   (2) INPUT: usb_hid binds the QEMU usb-tablet as a POINTER device.
#       This is the last driver to come up, so it also implies acpi ->
#       pci_decode -> platform -> pc_usb_host (xHCI) all came up
#       first.
#   (3) HOST-DRIVEN MULTI-PHASE CLICKS: after gates 1-2, sponge_de_probe
#       (inject=no, phases=input,panel,launch) emits QMP-TARGET click
#       markers on the serial console. The run script dispatches each
#       marker as a real QMP PS/2-mouse click (W3's proven recipe —
#       clamp-to-(0,0) + coarse rel-50 + fine rel-1, ±1px precision).
#       Between markers the probe polls capture for the effect (popup
#       open/close, green pixel, press report) — every poll is
#       bounded, fail loud on timeout.
#
#         input  — QMP click at the demo center → input report
#                  confirms press (criterion 1, Phase 10 W1).
#         panel  — QMP click S toggle (open popup); QMP click demo
#                  body (close popup via focus-out). Capture polls
#                  the popup rect for both transitions
#                  (criterion 4, Phase 10 W2).
#         launch — install pkg_gui_demo via the request channel
#                  (populates the launcher menu, mirrors
#                  run/sponge-launch.run's launch_probe); QMP click
#                  S (open popup); QMP click first launcher entry
#                  (launch). Capture polls for the green pixel +
#                  run-script gate on pkg_gui_demo's "window shown"
#                  marker for independent corroboration (criterion
#                  3, Phase 10 W2).
#       Every wait is bounded — fail loud, never hang.
#
run_genode_until {.*\[init -> drivers -> fb\] using 1024x768.*} 90
run_genode_until {.*Connected device.*QEMU QEMU USB Tablet.*POINTER.*} 90

#
# Gate 3 (host-driven QMP multi-phase click choreography). The Genode
# run tool respawns QEMU per run_genode_until call (power_cycle kills
# the prior QEMU), so we add the QMP socket arg to qemu_args ONLY here
# — gates 1 and 2 ran with a plain QEMU. qmp_pick_port returns a
# kernel-assigned ephemeral port (via `socket -server ... 0`) which is
# fresh per scenario invocation, dodging TIME_WAIT collisions.
#
# Single QEMU boot, single spawn. The probe runs an ordered phase list
# (input, panel, launch), each emitting QMP-TARGET click markers on
# the serial console. The host dispatches each marker via
# qmp_exec_target (bounded expect on qemu_spawn_id), which sends a
# real QMP PS/2-mouse click. Between markers the probe polls capture
# for the popup/green effect — every poll is bounded, fail loud.
#
#   (a) match "observe mode" — probe finished render poll, entering phases.
#   (b) qmp_connect — QEMU is alive, its QMP socket is up.
#   (c) phase input:  qmp_exec_target → demo-center click → wait for
#       "phase input PASS" (input report confirms press).
#   (d) phase panel:  qmp_exec_target → S-toggle click (open popup,
#                       bounded by probe's _poll_fraction);
#                     qmp_exec_target → demo-body click (close popup,
#                       bounded by probe's _poll_fraction);
#                     wait for "phase panel PASS".
#   (e) phase launch: install pkg_gui_demo (probe-driven via request
#                       channel; result ROM confirms ok);
#                     qmp_exec_target → S-toggle click (open popup);
#                     qmp_exec_target → first-entry click (launch);
#                     wait for pkg_gui_demo "window shown" marker
#                     (independent corroboration the launch chain ran);
#                     wait for "sponge-de-probe: phase launch PASS"
#                     (green pixel).
#   (f) final:         "sponge-de-probe: PASS" + "Run script execution
#                       successful".
#
set qmp_port [qmp_pick_port]
append qemu_args " -qmp tcp:127.0.0.1:${qmp_port},server=on,wait=off "

run_genode_until {.*sponge-de-probe: observe mode.*} 300

set qmp_chan [qmp_connect $qmp_port]
global qemu_spawn_id

#
# Phase input (criterion 1): QMP PS/2-mouse click at the demo center
# (512,412). The probe emits `QMP-TARGET click 512 412`; the host
# dispatches the click via qmp_ps2_click (clamp-to-(0,0) + coarse
# rel-50 + fine rel-1 → ±1px landing). The click propagates through
# the PS/2 → ps2 → event_filter → nitpicker → sponge-de chain, the
# press reaches the demo window, and sponge-de's input report
# confirms it. The run script gates on the probe's "phase input
# PASS" marker.
#
qmp_exec_target $qmp_chan 120
run_genode_until {.*sponge-de-probe: phase input PASS.*} 120 $qemu_spawn_id
after 500

#
# Phase panel (criterion 4): two QMP PS/2-mouse clicks — one on the
# S toggle (32,14) to open the popup, then one on the demo body
# (512,412) to close it via focus-out auto-close. The probe polls the
# popup rect (8,36,325,56) for non-bg fraction crossing the
# open/closed thresholds between the two clicks. FATAL — if either
# transition fails, the probe _fails and the run script's second
# qmp_exec_target will time out (no more markers arrive).
#
qmp_exec_target $qmp_chan 120
qmp_exec_target $qmp_chan 120
run_genode_until {.*sponge-de-probe: phase panel PASS.*} 120 $qemu_spawn_id
after 500

#
# Phase launch (criterion 3): the probe first installs pkg_gui_demo
# via the request channel (NOT a click-to-launch proof; this
# populates the launcher menu so the S popup has an entry to
# click — same install-via-request pattern as
# run/sponge-launch.run's launch_probe). After the install
# confirms, the probe emits two more QMP-TARGET tabclick markers
# (W4 tablet-abs recipe: mouse_set + abs move + HMP mouse_button
# — deterministic landing, see docs/evidence/task-4-phase10-
# interactive.md): the S toggle (32,14) to open the popup, then
# the first launcher entry (170,73) to launch. The full click-
# to-launch chain runs:
# Qt click → LauncherController::request_launch → launcher_request
# report → pkgd _do_launch → pkg_runtime config regen →
# pkg_gui_demo boot → green #00ff00 first paint. The probe's
# green-pixel poll catches the first paint; the run script
# additionally gates on pkg_gui_demo's "window shown" boot marker
# (independent corroboration, misleading_success_output defense).
# FATAL.
#
# The PS/2 QMP-TARGET click path is used for the input + panel
# phases (where the QTimer-based cursor-outside auto-close handles
# the popup hide). The launch phase switches to the W4 tablet-abs
# recipe because the PS/2 fine-walk accumulates 5-20px drift on
# a 60-event navigation (not enough for the 30px-tall entry
# button), while the W4 recipe lands exactly on the target
# pixel. The 600s timeout covers the ~1s tabclick dispatch
# (mouse_set + 1 abs move + 2 HMP mouse_button calls with
# 15ms inter-call delays).
#
qmp_exec_target $qmp_chan 120
qmp_exec_target $qmp_chan 120
run_genode_until {.*sponge-de-probe: phase launch PASS.*} 600 $qemu_spawn_id
after 500

#
# Independent corroboration gate (misleading_success_output defense):
# pkg_gui_demo must have logged its "window shown" boot marker at
# some point during the launch chain — the run script gates on it
# explicitly to make the corroboration visible (a green pixel alone
# could be a stale pixel; the boot marker is unambiguous).
#
run_genode_until {.*pkg_gui_demo: window shown.*} 60 $qemu_spawn_id

#
# Final gate.
#
run_genode_until {.*sponge-de-probe: PASS.*} 60 $qemu_spawn_id

qmp_disconnect $qmp_chan

#
# Interactive viewing (control escape hatch, AGENTS.md §1.1):
# This scenario is headless by design. To drive the desktop with a real
# mouse/keyboard, replace the gated run_genode_until lines above with
# `run_genode_until forever`, re-enable `QEMU_OPT += -display sdl` in
# build.conf (or drop -nographic above), and move the host mouse — it
# reaches the guest through the usb-tablet bound by usb_hid above (the
# PS/2 mouse used by QMP here is the same emulated controller the
# host's SDL mouse eventually feeds).
#
